A||||39||SpeedPhreak||? for those using ATF in clutch...||||5||
Z||000000||SpeedPhreak||11-24-1999||10:55 AM||||n e probs yet?  how is it holding up?  how did u get the dot3 out of the lines?  when bleeding did u follow the v8 archie instructions (ie pushing the rod into the slave cylinder)?  <P>my leg should be good enough to let me work on my car in the next day or two & by then all the snow should be pretty much gone (i live in colorado & we just got belted w/our 1st major storm).  <P>thanx<P>speedphreak||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||11-24-1999||07:51 PM||||I just repaired my clutch system today and used jack oil instead of brake fluid. I bled the system with the v8archie method by myself! It works great. . . for now.||||reg||14
Z||000002||mutfish||11-24-1999||09:28 PM||||I bench bled and installed a new master cylinder last week,flushed the line with atf,dissassembled the slave,cleaned it and filled the system with atf. SO far the clutch has a better"feel",and seems to have a much more consistent release.I also think the system is easier to bleed with atf.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mutfish||11-24-1999||10:28 PM||||By the way,since I was alone,I used a metal push broom handle to manipulate the clutch pedal while I leaned over the quarter panel to open and close the bleeder screw.<BR>Hey,maybe that's the start of a new topic....<BR>called, "It worked for me".||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick||11-24-1999||10:47 PM||||<BR>The use of <B>ATF</B> in my clutch hydraulics is by far the best thing I've EVER learned about ANYTHING on the 'net. I'm not kidding.<P>It's difficult to explain, but I used a vacuum pump to push ATF into my master cylinder and to force the old fluid out the other end. I know that sounds weird, but I had to get inventive because of the number of times I <B>used</B> to have to bleed my system.<P>Make sure the slave is completely emptied of Dot3. I didn't finish off my bleeding by pushing in the slave rod originally and I did have a problem one day. But follow the method that V8 Archie recommends and you'll be fine.<P>And by the way, suck the Dot3 and all the <B>muck</B> out of the reservoir BEFORE you start the bleeding.||||reg||14
Z||000005||David||11-25-1999||11:53 AM||||When I converted to Mobil 1, I took off the master cylinder and removed fluid. I then placed a plastic cup under the fitting that screws into the M/C.  Place some rags in the cup to control the splash.  Then I pushed the slave cyl piston to the botton and held it there.  Next I took out the bleeder screw and blew about 30 psi of air into the bleed screw hole and it removed all the old brake fluid.  Have someone hold the cup.  Bench bleed the M/C and but everything back on the car.  I used a vacuum pump to refill/bleed the system.  Used a total of about 1/4 quart (250cc), and never had to add any more.  The car was on a level surface without any of the tilting that I have read about.<p>[This message has been edited by David (edited 11-25-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||88GT||11-25-1999||12:15 PM||||Why does ATF fluid work better?||||reg||5
Z||000007||Patrick||11-25-1999||01:54 PM||||<BR>I believe it's because <B>ATF</B> is <B>thicker</B> than brake fluid, and this allows the rubber seals (especially in the slave) to work better at keeping air out of the system.||||reg||14
Z||000008||David||11-25-1999||04:05 PM||||My thought is that is thicker, allowing the pressure to increase faster in the master cylinder, causing the seals to seat faster, thus giving the slave cyl more cc's of fluid.  I started to use Mobil because if I pressed lightly on my clutch pedal, the pedal would go to the floor with no movement at the slave.  This was because there was fluid leaking past the M/C seals and back into the reservoir.  So in my case it was to fix a M/C problem.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phil||11-25-1999||05:09 PM||||Water, Thick oil, thin oil,ATF, jack oil cooking oil, all liquids are non-compressible  so it won't build pressure any faster- therefore you will have to look for another reason. Just be happy somebody came up with it.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||11-25-1999||05:57 PM||||OK guys,<BR>I bought brand new master and slave cylinders for my clutch.  They are still in the plastic bags.  Should I just start out on the right foot and put in ATF, or stick with DOT3?||||reg||5
Z||000011||Patrick||11-25-1999||07:12 PM||||<BR>Ummm... do you really have to ask? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000012||David||11-25-1999||07:22 PM||||Phil<BR>You're right about the compressability of fluids, until an orifice (the steel line) or a leak is in the system.  ATF and such covers up these leaks.  If a pressure gauge was somehow mounted to show the pressure in the bore of the M/C I think that it would be found that the cylinders with ATF have a higher pressure owing to the thicker fluid and the restriction in the line.  My own experience was that after I replaced my DOT3 fluid my clutch pedal was stiffer to depress, as if I had put in a clutch with higher spring pressure or had installed a stronger return spring on the pedal.  Since I did neither, the increase in pedal effort comes from higher pressure in the circuit.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phil||11-25-1999||08:09 PM||||You may want to review your fluid dynamics- a restriction (brake lines etc.) will effect the flow rate of the fluid resulting in a slower rate of pressure build up at the slave. Once the fluid stops moving the pressures will be equal through out the system. While you are pressurizing the system (stepping on the pedal) you will experience a higher pressure at the master for a short time,hence the harder pedal.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Patrick||11-25-1999||08:14 PM||||<BR>Wherever and however, it works great guys... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000015||Phil||11-25-1999||10:06 PM||||Correctomundo!||||reg||1
Z||000016||fiero56||11-29-1999||06:30 PM||||I'm ready to put in ATF into my clutch system.  What type do you recommend? The regular stuff, or synthetic? I know this question sounds dumb, but I don't want to mess with the clutch system for a long time.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Patrick||11-29-1999||07:06 PM||||<BR>Is there synthetic ATF? If there is and it's thinner (like the motor oils are), I wouldn't use it. I think in this case the thicker (within reason) the better.<P>I just used regular Dexron III ATF. Remember, this fluid is NOT operating under overly harsh conditions.||||reg||4
Z||000018||Ozzy||11-29-1999||08:51 PM||||Using ATF in my clutch system has been the LEAST of my problems with this car!<P>I dismantled both cyclinders completley mainly 'cause they were chocked full of muck. I then placed a tube directly onto the master cyclinder end hydraulic line. The tube was fixed into a big bottle of ATF and I placed a clear tube on the slave end hydraulic line. I then gravity fed the fresh ATF into the lines untill nothing but clean ATF was coming out the other end. After closing the bleed nimple at the slave I reattached the cylinders. I then bled the lines conventionally untill all air was removed. I also placed a new slave kit and used the old piston behind the new as some one else here suggested. It has worked faultlessly since. Not much more pedal but reliable.<P>Ozzy||||reg||12
Z||000019||fiero56||11-29-1999||09:54 PM||||I was at Pep Boys today getting other assorted parts, and thought I'd drop over to the fluids and get some ATF.  Then I saw durablend(valvoline's synthetic) and regular dexronIII/MerconV. I'll go with the regular stuff. Thanks.||||reg||14
Z||000020||2FAST4U||11-29-1999||10:18 PM||||I live in Denver also<P>I have lots of spare parts and I know where most of the junck yards are that have Fiero's in them are.<P>I am building a 87 show car now in my home shop and my next project is a 87 with a 430 CID Olds for drag racing at Bandimere.<P>Give me a call sometimes<P>Joe LeCroix<BR>3850 Sheridan Blvd.<BR>Denver, CO 80212<P>(303) 964-9066||||reg||1
Z||000021||Cooter||11-29-1999||10:25 PM||||It has been a few days since my conversion to the jack oil in the clutch system. I still believe that this car has never shifted any easier! No longer do you have to try to push the clutch pedal through the floor to get it to release. I wish that I had done this a long time ago.||||reg||14
Z||000022||Thomas||11-30-1999||08:31 AM||||A higher pressure in the Clutch tube and the cylinder will brake the Clutch system after a while. <P>The bigger fluid ATF will works.<BR>But after a while the seals begin to break.<BR>Hihger pressure in the tubes is higher pressure at the seals to. <BR>And there exist a wound point in the tube.<BR>Some inches behind the slave cyl. begins a flexible part in the tube.<BR>I KNOW 5 FIEROS WHERE THIS PART OF THE TUBE WAS CRACKED IN DRIVE. THE RESULT IS A BURNING CAR.<BR>At my car too,but without burning.<BR>The tube cracks between the connection of the hart metal part and the flexible part.<BR>Some people try to fix the crack with a gastube. I too.<BR>I leaned very fast: You can´t fix the crack at this way. I has done it at other way.<BR>At the first i cut out the flexible part of the tube.<BR>Then i build in flexible brake tube from Mercedes. This tubes are high Pressure tubes made in rubber. At both ends I screwed the  tubes together with screws like at the top of the slave cylinders.<P>ATF is a bigger fluid and need a little big more time through the back vent in the reservoir. <BR>But why they build in a regulation vent, if you need more pressure.<BR>Beleave me, give me 1$ for every minute i spend in my clutch and i can live in Honululu for the rest of my life. :-))<p>[This message has been edited by Thomas (edited 11-30-1999).]||||reg||11
Z||000023||Phil||11-30-1999||09:34 AM||||Think about this: The determining factor in pressure in the clutch hydraulic system is the resistance of the pressure plate springs.<BR>If the slave isn't pushing against anything you will have virtually no pressure in the system just like having the bleed screw open.Yes you will have some piping friction but that will be more funtion of pedal velocity(faster you push the pedal the harder it will seem).But like someone said eariler "WHO CARES WHY IT WORKS IT JUST DOES"<BR>Bye now!||||reg||1
Z||000024||Thomas||11-30-1999||10:51 AM||||Thats right.<BR>But DOT3 is more fluid then ATF, like water.<BR>If you reach the maximum pressure at the pressure plate the overpressure goes back in the reservoir. ATF need more time to flow back in the reservoir. For this time you have a over pressure in the tube. And that is the problem i mean. All overpressure it the dead for the clutch.<BR>You can get overpressure from differend ways:<BR>Wrong adjustment of the lever.<BR>Dirty in the system.<BR>To long rod at slave cylinder.<BR>Wrong fluid.<BR>Wrong or faulty clutch bearing.||||reg||1
Z||000025||Cooter||11-30-1999||05:06 PM||||The reason I used jack oil was because it was thin- it poured as easy as the brake fluid and seems to be just as thin. I checked this morning when it was below freezing and the oil still poured fine. I cant see how this will make a pressure hose fail.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Patrick||11-30-1999||06:28 PM||||<BR>Thomas, I appreciate your concerns, but it's not like we're all putting <B>90W gear oil</B> into our clutch hydraulic systems! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000027||theogre||11-30-1999||08:28 PM||||Why this works. Why the peddal feels stiffer. From this thread and others.<P>With ATF in the system you get:<P>1. a thicker oil. This will flow somewhat slower than DOT3 simply because of viscosity. You can test this yourself. cut a plastic bottle in half an poke a small hole in the cap or the base depending on your bottle. pour equal volumes of each into the bottle with the hole stoppered then release the hole and time how long it takes to drain. ((We use often this same method, with a calbrated cylider, to determin how much thinner we want in paint.)) Slower flow will make the peddal feal firmer. If you wanted an oil closer to DOT3 viscosity try WD40 or CRC 5-56, both available in non airosol. I'd like to try 5-56. It's less volotile than WD40. I have an auto tranny however, and my pinto used a cable clutch so I couldn't ever test it there either.<P>2. You get a more plastic friendly oil. ATF is designed to be good to the materials seals are made from.<P>3. ATF is a better lubricant, and does not attract water. Seals in these systems always leak a tiny bit. when DOT3 leaks out it draws water to the metal quickly causing corosion that eats the seals. When ATF leaks a tiny bit it simply lubricates the pistons.<P>Why you don't need DOTx fluid. Very simply the clutch oil will never see the heat the brakes do. Any of the alternate oils mentioned would fry in the brakes. Use only DOTx fluid in the brakes.<P>DOTx Fluid is used in the clutch mainly to stay uniform. It's easier to load both systems from one pump on the assembly line.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Eddie||12-02-1999||08:19 PM||||I have dot3 in mine. I have to say I love the way my clutch feels! may be your trying to make up for an old clutch. Abuot bleedind it can you replace the bleeder screw with one of the self bleeding screws they sell for brakes? ||||reg||6
